<h2>The decoder</h2>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Headless use</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The decoding pipeline has no dependency on Blazor or on a browser. It runs anywhere .NET
        runs, which is what makes it testable and benchmarkable.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">using var</span> decoder = <span class="k">new</span> BbBarcodeDecoder(<span class="k">new</span> BbScannerOptions
{
    Formats = BbBarcodeFormat.QrCode | BbBarcodeFormat.DataMatrix,
    DuplicateSuppressionWindow = TimeSpan.Zero,
});

<span class="k">var</span> result = decoder.Decode(rgbaPixels, width, height, BbPixelFormat.Rgba32);
<span class="k">if</span> (result <span class="k">is not null</span>)
{
    Console.WriteLine(<span class="s">$"{result.Format}: {result.Text}"</span>);
    Console.WriteLine(<span class="s">$"took {decoder.LastFrame.Total.TotalMilliseconds:0.00} ms"</span>);
}</pre>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead><tr><th>BbBarcodeDecoder member</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">new BbBarcodeDecoder(BbScannerOptions? options = null)</td><td>Builds the readers for <code>options.Formats</code>, once. Dispose it to return its pooled buffers.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Decode(pixels, width, height, format, suppressDuplicates = true)</td><td>The first symbol in packed pixels, or null.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">DecodeAll(pixels, width, height, format, maxSymbols = 16, suppressDuplicates = true)</td><td>Every symbol, matrix symbols first.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Decode(in BbLuminanceView view, ...) / DecodeAll(in BbLuminanceView view, ...)</td><td>The same, from a frame that is already 8 bit grayscale.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">LastFrame</td><td>The <code>BbScanDiagnostics</code> of the last call.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">FramesDecoded, FramesWithSymbol</td><td>Counters since the decoder was created.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">DuplicateSuppressionWindow, ResetDuplicateFilter()</td><td>Its own duplicate filter, changeable while running; pass <code>suppressDuplicates: false</code> to bypass it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">Options</td><td>The options it was created with.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A decoder is not thread safe: give each thread its own. Every result carries the same
        <code>BbBarcodeResult</code> the component reports.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Building blocks</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Every reader implements <code>IBbRowDecoder</code> for a linear code or
        <code>IBbMatrixDecoder</code> for a two dimensional one, and can be driven directly by a
        pipeline of your own. <code>BbBarcodeDecoder</code> composes the built-in readers from
        <code>Formats</code> and does not accept others.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">public interface</span> IBbRowDecoder
{
    BbBarcodeFormat Formats { <span class="k">get</span>; }
    BbSymbolDecodeResult? DecodeRow(<span class="k">int</span> rowNumber, BbBitRow row, BbBarcodeFormat enabledFormats);
}

<span class="k">public interface</span> IBbMatrixDecoder
{
    BbBarcodeFormat Formats { <span class="k">get</span>; }
    BbSymbolDecodeResult? Decode(BbBitMatrix image, BbMatrixDecodeOptions options);
}</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Binarisation follows the same shape: <code>IBbBinarizer</code> has one implementation tuned for
        matrix codes, one for linear rows and a global histogram fallback, each usable on its own.
    </p>
</div>
